    "created_at": "2015-12-04T04:14:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-12-04T04:14:54Z",
    "body": "Hmm.  So IDL uses \"ECMAScript global environment\" to refer to the thing of the same name mentioned in http://www.ecma-international.org/ecma-262/6.0/#sec-code-realms\n\nHTML has this bit:\n\n> The term JavaScript global environment refers to the global environment concept defined in the ECMAScript specification.\n\nwhich presumably refers to the same thing.\n\nAnyway, that's the theory.  On to the practice...\n\nFor the `DrawingStyles` case, the intent seems pretty clearly to be to examine the \"JavaScript global environment\" corresponding to the Realm of the constructor.  That's what your phrasing is getting at as well, except the concept of \"foo's global object\" is not really defined anywhere for some function \"foo\".  We could clearly define it as the global of the function's Realm, either in IDL, or in ES, or over here.  IDL right now uses the phrasing \"the ECMAScript global environment associated with foo\" when it means this concept.\n\nBy the way, the part that actually says what you do with this global once you find it seems totally daft to me.  Filed https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/382 on that.\n\nFor the `CanvasRenderingContext2D` case, I would think the intent is to reference the global environment the object is associated with, as defined in Web IDL section 4.7:\n\n>  Every platform object is associated with a global environment, just as the initial objects are. It is the responsibility of specifications using Web IDL to state which global environment (or, by proxy, which global object) each platform object is associated with. \n\nThere are some open issues to have Web IDL define a default association in more cases, but in this case that's not even needed.  The only ways to get hold of a `CanvasRenderingContext2D` are via calling its constructor or via calling `getContext()` on a `<canvas>`.  If you do it via `getContext()` you get a `CanvasRenderingContext2D` whose context bitmap mode is fixed, so we don't even get to all this global environment stuff.  For the ones created via calling the constructor, this part of Web IDL defines the global environment the return value is associated with:\n\n>  If the internal [[Call]] method of the interface object returns normally, then it MUST return an object that implements interface I. This object also MUST be associated with the ECMAScript global environment associated with the interface object. \n\n (This text is hiding in the depths of http://heycam.github.io/webidl/#es-interface-call below all the overload resolution gunk that we should really factor out into a separate section or something.)\n\nDoes that help?\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
